WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.930
Arguably the most natural&nbsp;
and powerful form of learning

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:04.980
is through experience,

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:09.090
or more precisely through reflection on doing.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.850
Also called experiential learning,

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:13.980
it’s what prima ballerinas do

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.380
after their performance at the national opera.

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:19.230
But it also happens to boys that are sad

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:20.910
because their father got angry

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.130
when they played football in the living room.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:26.070
By the age of one,

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.680
we all had our own painful encounter with

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:29.760
experiential learning

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.040
when we tried to walk, failed,

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.610
fell and cried like, well a baby…

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.910
And even though this was an unpleasant and

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.430
discouraging exercise that lasted for months

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:42.570
in the end

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:43.590
we all made it.

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:45.630
How is that possible?

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:50.340
As soon as we fell and the first shock was over,

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.700
our brain unconsciously began to make sense

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.170
out of all of the information available

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:58.020
to identify how this embarrassment occurred.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.230
It remembers that when we pushed ourselves up,

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:02.910
everything was fine:

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:04.230
our feet on the floor,

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.870
our arms in position and our head

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:08.160
and shoulders up right.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.600
Ready to go!

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.110
When our upper leg muscles

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:16.200
pulled our left foot 12.3% to the front

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.060
at an angle of 23 degree,

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.880
our arms didn’t compliment the movement

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.890
and the ventricles in the inner ear,

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:25.110
responsible for static balance,

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:26.790
got confused for a second.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:29.760
When at the same moment the cat ran by,

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.090
our eyes sent an alarming&nbsp;
signal to the hippocampus

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:34.710
and we completely lost it…

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:35.730
Outch!

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:40.800
Unconsciously this is how our brain analyses

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.260
the relationship of events within our body

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:44.430
or in the environment.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:48.120
It happens all the time as we learn to walk,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:48.930
talk,

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:49.710
kiss,

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.510
function in a fancy office

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.580
or dance the salsa.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.910
Once we understand the connections between

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:57.750
what went wrong,

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.550
we know what we need to change

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:00.900
when we try the next time.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:04.200
Experiential Learning

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.170
can also be used explicitly to learn a new skill

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:10.290
or to become better at what we already love doing.

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.240
Here is how it works:

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.620
First get yourself into a situation to experience.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.530
After, reflect on what happened.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:22.800
Then try to understand the relationships

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.090
to form an abstract concept

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:25.770
- if I do A,

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.030
I get B.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:30.870
Last, decide what to do differently next time.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:32.490
Then do it again.

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:38.520
Experiential learning is also&nbsp;
believed to be responsible

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:39.450
for the fact that musicians

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:42.090
generally fare better at most tests,

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:43.710
regardless of what they measure.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:46.620
People that practice an instrument

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.660
not only engage their brain in motor,

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.850
visual and auditory areas,

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.760
but they also learn by&nbsp;
reflecting on what they’re doing

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.380
with a fast feedback loop

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:59.130
– a wrong tone on the violin sounds too terrible

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:00.060
to remain unnoticed.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:05.100
While playing they therefore&nbsp;
not only learn to make music,

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.580
but also that progress in&nbsp;
general comes through practice,

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:09.570
reflection,

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:10.740
understanding,

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:11.940
and repetition.

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:15.390
You can use it with your friends

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.010
or colleagues when working on a project.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.650
Silicon Valley start-ups do it

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:22.080
when they tell their developers&nbsp;
to get out of the building!

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:25.860
After the interaction with&nbsp;
real potential customers,

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:27.000
the team gets together,

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.920
analyses the feedback

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.570
and decides what to do next.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:34.510
Tell us, what do you think?

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:37.210
Is learning through reflecting on doing

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:40.090
only good when acquiring new hands-on skills

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:43.090
or is it also suitable to study science,

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:43.330
math,

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:44.980
the humanities

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:46.330
or abstract art?

